<section class="tk-hero"><div class="tk-w"><p class="tk-tag">Think clearly &middot; Decide well</p><h1>Good thinking is a skill.<br><span>You can practise it.</span></h1><div class="tk-hero-g"><p class="tk-lead">Thinker is an independent study site about reasoning. Learn to test a claim, judge a source and work through a problem one step at a time, with three small tools to practise on.</p><p class="tk-acts"><a class="btn" href="#tools">Practise now</a><a class="tk-ln" href="critical-thinking-guide.html">Start with the guide</a></p></div><figure class="tk-hero-f"><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1769738360873-3ba6cac0b308?auto=format&fit=crop&w=1300&h=520&q=75" alt="woman drawing a flowchart on a whiteboard" width="1300" height="520" fetchpriority="high"></figure></div></section>

<section class="tk-s"><div class="tk-w"><p class="tk-tag">Four questions</p><h2>Ask these of any claim</h2><div class="tk-q4"><div><b>?</b><h3>What exactly is being said?</h3><p>Put it in one plain sentence. Vague claims cannot be tested.</p></div><div><b>&#8679;</b><h3>What supports it?</h3><p>Data, a study, an example, or just someone&rsquo;s confidence?</p></div><div><b>&#8644;</b><h3>What else could explain it?</h3><p>List at least one alternative before you decide.</p></div><div><b>&#10007;</b><h3>What would change my mind?</h3><p>If nothing could, it is a belief, not a conclusion.</p></div></div></div></section>

<section class="tk-s tk-ink" id="tools"><div class="tk-w"><p class="tk-tag">Thinking tools</p><h2>Three exercises, ready to use</h2><div class="tk-tools">
<div class="tk-tool"><h3>Source Checker</h3><p>Answer five questions about any article or post.</p><div class="tk-qs" id="sr-q"><label><input type="checkbox" value="author"> The author is named and has relevant expertise</label><label><input type="checkbox" value="evidence"> It cites evidence I can check</label><label><input type="checkbox" value="date"> It is recent enough for the topic</label><label><input type="checkbox" value="tone"> The tone is measured, not outraged</label><label><input type="checkbox" value="agree"> Other independent sources agree</label></div><label class="tk-sel" for="sr-m">Who benefits if I believe it?<select id="sr-m"><option value="0">No obvious interest</option><option value="1">It is selling something or campaigning</option></select></label><div class="tk-out" id="sr-o" aria-live="polite"></div></div>
<div class="tk-tool"><h3>Fallacy Spotter</h3><p>Pick the argument you heard. See what is wrong with it.</p><label class="tk-sel" for="fs-a">The argument goes&hellip;<select id="fs-a"><option value="adhom">&ldquo;You can&rsquo;t trust her view; look at how she dresses.&rdquo;</option><option value="cause">&ldquo;I started wearing this bracelet and my headaches stopped.&rdquo;</option><option value="band">&ldquo;Millions of people use it, so it must work.&rdquo;</option><option value="false">&ldquo;Either you support the plan, or you don&rsquo;t care about the town.&rdquo;</option><option value="straw">&ldquo;She wants shorter lunch breaks. So she thinks staff should starve.&rdquo;</option><option value="slope">&ldquo;If we allow phones in class, soon nobody will learn anything.&rdquo;</option><option value="auth">&ldquo;A famous actor says this diet works, so it does.&rdquo;</option></select></label><div class="tk-out" id="fs-o" aria-live="polite"></div></div>
<div class="tk-tool"><h3>Problem Breakdown</h3><p>Turn a vague problem into steps.</p><label class="tk-sel" for="pb-t">The problem is mainly about<select id="pb-t"><option value="time">Not enough time</option><option value="study">A subject I don&rsquo;t understand</option><option value="decision">A decision I can&rsquo;t make</option><option value="group">A group that can&rsquo;t agree</option><option value="stuck">A task I keep putting off</option></select></label><label class="tk-sel" for="pb-d">Deadline<select id="pb-d"><option value="day">Within a day</option><option value="week" selected>Within a week</option><option value="month">A month or more</option></select></label><div class="tk-out" id="pb-o" aria-live="polite"></div></div>
</div></div></section>

<section class="tk-s"><div class="tk-w tk-board"><figure><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1769731738826-a51acd5bddc7?auto=format&fit=crop&w=600&h=620&q=75" alt="hand drawing a diagram on a whiteboard" width="600" height="620" loading="lazy"></figure><div><p class="tk-tag">A method that travels</p><h2>Claim, evidence, reasoning</h2><p>Teachers use this simple frame from primary school to university because it works on any subject.</p><dl class="tk-cer"><div><dt>Claim</dt><dd>One sentence that answers the question.</dd></div><div><dt>Evidence</dt><dd>The specific facts, data or examples that support it.</dd></div><div><dt>Reasoning</dt><dd>Why that evidence actually supports that claim.</dd></div></dl><p>Most weak arguments skip the third step. The evidence is real, but nobody explains why it proves the point.</p></div></div></section>

<section class="tk-s tk-lime"><div class="tk-w"><p class="tk-tag">Watch for these</p><h2>Six thinking traps</h2><div class="tk-traps"><article><h3>Confirmation bias</h3><p>Noticing only the evidence that fits what you already believe.</p></article><article><h3>Anchoring</h3><p>Leaning too heavily on the first number or idea you hear.</p></article><article><h3>Availability</h3><p>Judging how common something is by how easily examples come to mind.</p></article><article><h3>Sunk cost</h3><p>Continuing because of what you have already spent, not what lies ahead.</p></article><article><h3>Overconfidence</h3><p>Being more certain than the evidence allows.</p></article><article><h3>Groupthink</h3><p>Agreeing to keep the peace instead of saying what you see.</p></article></div></div></section>

<section class="tk-s"><div class="tk-w"><p class="tk-tag">Read in full</p><h2>Two guides</h2><div class="tk-gds"><a href="critical-thinking-guide.html"><div class="tk-gd-t"><span>Guide 1</span><h3>The Critical Thinking Guide</h3><p>Evaluating claims and sources, common fallacies and how to change your mind well.</p></div><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1758685848061-3080d0780285?auto=format&fit=crop&w=640&h=360&q=75" alt="woman looking at a blackboard with mathematics" width="640" height="360" loading="lazy"></a><a href="problem-solving-guide.html"><div class="tk-gd-t"><span>Guide 2</span><h3>The Problem Solving Guide</h3><p>Defining the problem, finding causes, generating options and testing the best one.</p></div><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1777261905002-1213c6d30f2a?auto=format&fit=crop&w=640&h=360&q=75" alt="close-up of a partly completed jigsaw puzzle" width="640" height="360" loading="lazy"></a></div></div></section>

<section class="tk-s tk-soft"><div class="tk-w tk-prac"><div><p class="tk-tag">Ten minutes a day</p><h2>A practice routine</h2><ol class="tk-ol"><li><b>Pick one headline.</b> Write the claim in your own words.</li><li><b>Find the evidence.</b> Follow at least one link back to its source.</li><li><b>Name an alternative.</b> What else could explain the same facts?</li><li><b>Rate your confidence.</b> Low, medium or high, and why.</li><li><b>Check back later.</b> Were you right? What did you miss?</li></ol></div><figure><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1763575647131-fea35b69e7d1?auto=format&fit=crop&w=560&h=640&q=75" alt="woman writing on paper with a pencil outdoors" width="560" height="640" loading="lazy"></figure></div></section>

<section class="tk-s"><div class="tk-w tk-fq"><div><p class="tk-tag">FAQ</p><h2>Good questions</h2></div><div class="tk-faq"><details open><summary>What is critical thinking?</summary><p>The habit of asking what a claim means, what evidence supports it and what would change your mind, before accepting or rejecting it. It is less about being sceptical of everything and more about matching your confidence to the evidence.</p></details><details><summary>How can I tell if a source is reliable?</summary><p>Ask who wrote it, what they know about the subject, whether they cite evidence you can check, when it was published and whether they gain from you believing it. A reliable source does well on most of those questions, not just one.</p></details><details><summary>What is a logical fallacy?</summary><p>A pattern of reasoning that sounds convincing but does not actually support its conclusion. Attacking the person instead of the argument, or assuming that because two things happened together one caused the other, are common examples.</p></details><details><summary>Can critical thinking be taught?</summary><p>Yes. It improves with practice on real examples, especially when you explain your reasoning aloud or in writing and get feedback. Short, regular practice works better than reading about it once.</p></details><details><summary>What is the first step in solving a problem?</summary><p>Defining it precisely. Many problems are hard because they are vague. Writing one clear sentence about what is wrong, and what &ldquo;solved&rdquo; would look like, often makes the next step obvious.</p></details><details><summary>How do I avoid confirmation bias?</summary><p>Actively look for the strongest evidence against what you believe, and ask what result would prove you wrong. If you cannot imagine any, the belief is not being tested.</p></details></div></div></section>

<section class="tk-nl"><div class="tk-w tk-nl-in"><div><h2>The Thinker Note</h2><p>One fallacy, one bias and one puzzle each month. Five minutes to read.</p></div><a class="btn" href="mailto:hello@thinker.com?subject=The%20Thinker%20Note&amp;body=Please%20subscribe%20me.">Subscribe by email</a></div></section>

